    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> wrote of the Redemption of humanity in the vernacular rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> one might expect, and the poem helped establish Tuscan as the standardized Italian language.</li><li>Its realms follow a pattern of nine plus one and its verse a pattern of threes, and its thought draws on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> and the Vulgate, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s exile from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> runs through it.</li><li>It has one of the most extensive critical traditions of any work of literature, with line-by-line commentaries across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and it has been translated into English more times than into any other language.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Each of the three cantiche ends on the same word, stelle (“stars”), and each tercet of terza rima has 33 syllables, the number of cantos in each cantica.</p></aside>
    </section>

